# Eleven right programs, and nothing leaves compiled code in any of them. A class object gets a name of its own that a class of its row declares as a static method, while a class of the same row has a static field under that name.
# Link lets the stored value flow into the variable of the field and leaves the method as it is, and the walk finds no static member without a look where a class of the row may have a static field under the name. So the store breaks no proof, and the run time lets a change of a class object through where a class of its row has a static field under the changed name.
# The rows in order: the field between the store and the method . the store is never run . no field in the row, so the method is stored over in sight . the field above the method . the store at the top level . a definition by a descriptor . the store on the class that has the field . an accessor in the row . the same on the side of the prototype objects, with a field of the objects . the store over the method itself, the field above it . the field above the method, and nobody calls through the row.
# The first row in TypeScript: store-on-a-class-object-of-a-row-with-a-static-field-goes-through-in-typescript.ir.
